course: dictionary
lesson: 10874
title: The Roman numeral VII
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: VII is a Roman numeral. Roman numerals use letters instead of our normal digits. V means 5 and I means 1. When a letter is written after a bigger one, you add them: V + I + I = 5 + 1 + 1 = 7. So VII means 7.
explain: VII is used for names of kings, queens and popes, so you say the number as a word. «King Edward VII» is said «Edward the Seventh». Look for the letters and count them up, just as a fact to check your answer.
explain: You will meet VII in other places too: on some clock faces, in the numbers of chapters and books, in the titles of film sequels, and in history lessons. When you see VII, read it as seven, and check that the answer makes sense in the sentence.
fact: VII is the Roman numeral for 7.
fact: V is 5, and I is 1, so VII is 5 + 1 + 1 = 7.
fact: Kings, queens and popes are often named with Roman numerals, such as Henry VII.
fact: Henry VII is said «Henry the Seventh».
fact: The name «The Wind in the Willows» came out in 1908, when «King Edward VII» was on the throne.
exercise: «Cleopatra VII was a queen of Egypt.» What number does VII stand for? | 2 | *7 | 5 | 12
exercise: How do you say Henry VII out loud? | Henry the Seventy | *Henry the Seventh | Henry the Eleventh | Henry the Second
exercise: Work out VII. V means 5 and I + I means 2. What is the total? | 12 | 2 | *7 | 5
test: Which Roman numeral means 7? | IV | *VII | VI | VIII
test: In «King Edward VII», what kind of person is Edward? | A footballer | *A king | A river | A tree
exam: A film is called Adventure VII. What number is the sequel? | 8 | 6 | 17 | *7
